<h2>Geometric Group Theory — the Cayley Graph, Hyperbolic Groups & the Growth of Groups</h2>
<p>Geometric Group Theory studies finitely generated groups as geometric objects. The central idea: a group <strong>G</strong> with a generating set <strong>S</strong> gives a metric space — the <strong>Cayley graph</strong> Cay(G,S) — where vertices are group elements and edges connect g to g·s. The <strong>word metric</strong> d(g,h) equals the minimal length of a word representing g<sup>−1</sup>h. Different generating sets yield <strong>quasi-isometric</strong> Cayley graphs, so geometric properties (growth type, hyperbolicity) are group invariants. <strong>Gromov's theorem</strong>: groups of polynomial growth are virtually nilpotent. <strong>Hyperbolic groups</strong> (Gromov, 1987) are those whose Cayley graph is δ-hyperbolic — geodesic triangles are δ-thin. The <strong>Dehn function</strong> measures the isoperimetric complexity; hyperbolic groups have linear Dehn functions, giving linear-time solvable word problems. <strong>CAT(0) cube complexes</strong> (Haglund-Wise, Agol) led to the resolution of the virtual Haken conjecture. The <strong>boundary at infinity</strong> of a hyperbolic group encodes its dynamics at large scale.</p>
<div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"A group is a geometric object."</div><div class="attribution">— Mikhail Gromov</div></div>
<div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div>
<h2>📏 Cayley Graphs and Growth of Groups</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Growth</div><div class="story-title">Cayley Graphs, the Word Metric, and the Growth Function</div><div class="story-text">For a finitely generated group G with a finite symmetric generating set S (S = S<sup>−1</sup>, 1 ∉ S), the <strong>Cayley graph</strong> Cay(G,S) has vertex set G and an oriented edge from g to g·s labeled by s ∈ S. This is a regular graph of degree |S|. The <strong>word metric</strong> d(g,h) = min{ℓ : g<sup>−1</sup>h = s₁⋯s_ℓ, s_i ∈ S} makes G into a proper geodesic metric space. The <strong>growth function</strong> γ_{G,S}(n) = |{g ∈ G : d(1,g) ≤ n}| counts elements in the ball of radius n. Growth type is independent of S up to quasi-isometry: polynomial (∃ C,d such that γ(n) ≤ C n^d), exponential (γ(n) ≥ a^n for some a > 1), or intermediate (neither polynomial nor exponential). <strong>Gromov's theorem</strong> (1981): a finitely generated group has polynomial growth iff it is <strong>virtually nilpotent</strong> — contains a nilpotent subgroup of finite index. The <strong>Grigorchuk group</strong> (1984), the first example of intermediate growth, is an infinite 2-group generated by 4 automorphisms of the rooted binary tree. <strong>Bass-Guivarc'h formula</strong>: for a nilpotent group, γ(n) ∼ n^d where d = Σ k·rank(G_k/G_{k+1}) is the <strong>Hirsch length</strong> (the sum of ranks of the quotients in the lower central series). The growth of free groups is exponential: for F_r, γ(n) = 1 + 2r·((2r−1)^n − 1)/(2r−2).</div><div class="story-moral">The growth function measures how fast a group expands; its asymptotic type (polynomial, exponential, intermediate) is a quasi-isometry invariant with deep algebraic consequences.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div>
<h2>🌍 Hyperbolic Groups</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Hyperbolic</div><div class="story-title">δ-Hyperbolic Spaces, Gromov's Definition, and the Boundary at Infinity</div><div class="story-text">A geodesic metric space X is <strong>δ-hyperbolic</strong> if every geodesic triangle Δ = [x,y] ∪ [y,z] ∪ [z,x] satisfies the δ-thin condition: each side is contained in the δ-neighborhood of the union of the other two sides. A finitely generated group G is <strong>hyperbolic</strong> if its Cayley graph (with any finite generating set) is δ-hyperbolic for some δ ≥ 0 — this is independent of the generating set up to quasi-isometry. Examples: free groups F_n (their Cayley graphs are trees, δ = 0), surface groups π₁(Σ_g) for g ≥ 2, fundamental groups of closed hyperbolic n-manifolds, co-compact lattices in Isom(ℍⁿ). Properties: hyperbolic groups are <strong>finitely presented</strong>, have a <strong>solvable word problem</strong> in linear time (the Dehn algorithm), and every element of infinite order has exponential growth. The <strong>Gromov boundary</strong> ∂G: the set of equivalence classes of geodesic rays (two rays are equivalent if they stay within bounded Hausdorff distance). For a non-elementary hyperbolic group, ∂G is a compact metrizable space. For F₂, ∂F₂ is the <strong>Cantor set</strong>. For surface groups, ∂π₁(Σ_g) is a circle S¹. For a hyperbolic 3-manifold group, ∂G ≅ S². The group acts on its boundary as a <strong>convergence group</strong> — a minimal, uniform convergence action. Quasi-isometries between hyperbolic groups induce homeomorphisms of boundaries. The <strong>Rips complex</strong> construction: for sufficiently large R, the Rips complex P_R(G) is contractible and G acts properly and cocompactly — giving a finite model for EG.</div><div class="story-moral">Hyperbolicity is a coarse negative-curvature condition: the Cayley graph looks like a negatively curved space at large scales, forcing strong algorithmic and structural properties.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div>
<h2>🧮 The Word Problem and the Dehn Function</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Isoperimetry</div><div class="story-title">The Word Problem, Van Kampen Diagrams, and the Dehn Function</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>word problem</strong> for a finitely presented group G = ⟨S | R⟩: given a word w in the free group F(S), decide whether w = 1 in G. A <strong>van Kampen diagram</strong> is a planar, simply connected 2-complex whose boundary label is w and whose 2-cells are labeled by relators in R — it provides a geometric proof that w = 1. The <strong>Dehn function</strong> (or isoperimetric function) φ(n) = max_{w = 1, |w| ≤ n} min{Area(D) : D is a van Kampen diagram for w}. A group is <strong>hyperbolic</strong> iff its Dehn function is <strong>linear</strong> (φ(n) ≤ C·n). The <strong>Dehn algorithm</strong> solves the word problem for hyperbolic groups in linear time: scan the word for a subword that is more than half of a relator; if found, replace it by the shorter side; repeat; if no reduction exists, the word is geodesic and non-trivial. The <strong>isoperimetric spectrum</strong>: Dehn functions can be polynomial (e.g., n² for Baumslag-Solitar BS(1,2)), exponential, or even super-exponential. <strong>Automatic groups</strong> (Epstein, Cannon, Thurston) are groups with a regular language of normal forms such that the multiplication by a generator is a synchronous regular relation. Hyperbolic groups are automatic: the set of geodesic words forms an automatic structure. The <strong>asynchronous automatic groups</strong> and <strong>biautomatic groups</strong> generalize this. A group is <strong>combable</strong> if there is a language of quasi-geodesic paths satisfying the fellow-traveler property. The <strong>word problem is solvable</strong> for any automatic group in quadratic time. The <strong>conjugacy problem</strong> for hyperbolic groups is also solvable (in polynomial time via the geodesic core). The <strong>small cancellation theory</strong> (C′(1/6) condition) gives explicit examples of hyperbolic groups via presentations where overlaps between relators are limited.</div><div class="story-moral">The Dehn function captures the complexity of the word problem geometrically: hyperbolic groups have the simplest possible isoperimetric behavior — a linear Dehn function.</div></div>
<div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div>
<h2>🔥 CAT(0) Cube Complexes and the Haglund-Wise Theory</h2>
<div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Cubulation</div><div class="story-title">CAT(0) Spaces, Cube Complexes, and the Virtual Haken Conjecture</div><div class="story-text">A <strong>CAT(0) metric space</strong> is a geodesic metric space where every geodesic triangle is at least as thin as the corresponding Euclidean comparison triangle. A <strong>CAT(0) cube complex</strong> is a cube complex (built from unit cubes [0,1]ⁿ glued along faces) with the CAT(0) metric — it is simply connected and non-positively curved. The <strong>Sageev's construction</strong> (1995): a group acting on a CAT(0) cube complex yields a <strong>wall space</strong> structure (a set of half-spaces with finite width). A group is <strong>cubulated</strong> if it acts properly and cocompactly on a CAT(0) cube complex. The <strong>Haglund-Wise theorem</strong> (2007): hyperbolic groups that are cubulated are <strong>virtually special</strong> — they contain a finite-index subgroup that embeds into a right-angled Artin group (RAAG). Virtually special groups are <strong>residually finite</strong>, have the <strong>Haagerup property</strong> (a-T-menability), and satisfy the <strong>Novikov conjecture</strong>. The <strong>Agol's theorem</strong> (2012, the virtual Haken conjecture): every closed hyperbolic 3-manifold has a finite cover that is Haken — equivalently, its fundamental group is cubulated. This used the <strong>Wise's theorem</strong> (2009): a hyperbolic group with a <strong>quasiconvex hierarchy</strong> is cubulated. A <strong>quasiconvex hierarchy</strong> is a way to build a group by repeatedly splitting over quasiconvex subgroups (analogous to Haken hierarchies in 3-manifold theory). Applications: the <strong>virtual fibering conjecture</strong> (proved by Agol): every closed hyperbolic 3-manifold has a finite cover that fibers over S¹. The <strong>residual finiteness</strong> of hyperbolic groups: cubulated hyperbolic groups are residually finite. The <strong>Novikov conjecture</strong> (the homotopy invariance of higher signatures) holds for groups acting properly and cocompactly on CAT(0) cube complexes. The <strong>Bartels-Lück theorem</strong>: the Farrell-Jones conjecture holds for CAT(0) groups and hyperbolic groups. The <strong>Stallings' theorem on ends of groups</strong>: a finitely generated group has more than one end iff it splits over a finite subgroup (an amalgamated product or HNN extension over a finite subgroup). The <strong>Dunwoody's accessibility theorem</strong>: finitely presented groups can be split only finitely many times over finite subgroups.</div><div class="story-moral">The cubulation program — showing hyperbolic groups act on CAT(0) cube complexes — revolutionized 3-manifold topology and proved the virtual Haken conjecture, establishing residual finiteness and the Novikov conjecture for large classes of groups.</div></div>
